Rust
====

url    = https://github.com/rust-lang/rust.git
tag    = 1.99.1
commit = 58cf8f9c35b355199727627d07ae34a4e2846d67
date   = 2026-10-13

tools/linux/build-rust.sh (tools/linux/build.sh rust) cross-builds this
release on a Linux host into Rust's dist tarballs for
x86_64-unknown-dragonfly: rustc, cargo, rust-std, rust-src, rustfmt and
clippy, with prefix /System/Library/Rust.  It builds from the release's
rustc-1.99.1-src.tar.xz (static.rust-lang.org, checked by SHA-256),
which is this tag plus what the build needs and the repository does
not carry: the vendored crates and LLVM.  The submodule records the
revision; nothing is built from it, and its own submodules are not
fetched.  Rust publishes no toolchain that runs on DragonFly (a tier 3
target), and DPorts' (1.85) is older than current programs need.

Changes made in the build directory, not in this submodule:
- cargo is built native-static (OpenSSL, curl and libgit2 inside, as
  Rust's own dists): the base LibreSSL is private.
- the vendored openssl-src crate configures OpenSSL with
  no-devcryptoeng on DragonFly, which has no /dev/crypto; its
  .cargo-checksum.json entry is updated to match.
- the target's C and C++ drivers (xcc) link C++ against gcc 12's
  libstdc++ (/usr/lib/gcc120) with that run path, before gcc 8's.

License: MIT OR Apache-2.0 for Rust itself (LICENSE-MIT,
LICENSE-APACHE, COPYRIGHT), with the licences listed in COPYRIGHT and
LICENSES/ for LLVM (Apache-2.0 WITH LLVM-exception) and the vendored
crates.  The dist tarballs carry them.  Complete the file-level and
provenance review before distributing the toolchain.

When upgrading, update the version and checksum in build-rust.sh, check
that the openssl-src change still applies, and run rustc and cargo in
the guest (docs/rust.md).
